Here is your updated methodology section, ready for inclusion.
2. Proposed Methodology
The proposed framework introduces an Adaptive Semantic Intelligence Layer (ASIL) between the Automatic Speech Recognition (ASR) system and the downstream Large Language Model (LLM). Unlike conventional cascaded pipelines, where the ASR transcript is directly forwarded for reasoning, ASIL performs lightweight semantic understanding, intent-guided candidate generation, and semantic reconciliation. Crucially, while this framework leverages existing shared-encoder paradigms for foundational metadata, its core novel contribution is a deterministic, sub-utterance uncertainty routing mechanism and an Attention-based Evidence Fusion Network. The complete framework is designed as an inference-time semantic correction layer deployable on edge hardware without retraining the underlying foundation models.
Step 1. Shared Speech Encoding
The input speech waveform is first processed using the Whisper-Large-v3-Turbo encoder. The Whisper model itself is not fine-tuned, ensuring that the framework remains domain-independent. The encoder generates contextual acoustic representations of the complete speech utterance. During deployment, the Whisper decoder simultaneously generates the speech transcript while the same encoder embeddings are reused by ASIL, eliminating redundant speech encoding. For future domain adaptation, optional LoRA/Adapter tuning may be trained independently while keeping the base Whisper encoder frozen.
Step 2. Lightweight Shared NLU Head
The shared Whisper encoder output is simultaneously forwarded to a lightweight multi-task Natural Language Understanding (NLU) module. This NLU consists of a small feed-forward neural network operating directly on the Whisper encoder embeddings. The representations are mean-pooled to obtain a fixed-dimensional speech embedding, which is passed through a Multi-Layer Perceptron (Linear \rightarrow GELU/ReLU \rightarrow Dropout \rightarrow Linear). Multiple classification heads predict semantic properties including Domain, Intent, Dialogue Act, Urgency, Entity Type, and an NLU Confidence Score. During inference, only a single forward pass is required, generating the foundational metadata that drives the subsequent novel routing components.
Step 3. ASR Confidence Estimation
Simultaneously with transcript generation, token-level confidence scores are extracted directly from the Whisper decoder's log probabilities generated during beam search. For every decoded token, the posterior probability is converted into a normalized confidence score between 0 and 1. Tokens failing to meet a baseline confidence threshold are flagged as uncertain semantic regions and become candidates for targeted correction.
Step 4. Intent-Aware Context-Guided Candidate Generation
For every low-confidence token, candidate generation is performed using three complementary retrieval mechanisms: Embedding Similarity via approximate nearest-neighbor search (FAISS), Phonetic Similarity via edit-distance algorithms (e.g., RapidFuzz), and Character Similarity. Rather than relying on unconstrained retrieval, the search space is dynamically restricted using the intent and domain predictions from the NLU module. For example, if the NLU predicts "Test Discussion" within the "Medical" domain, the search is constrained strictly to medical diagnostic tests. This bounds the retrieval space, dramatically reducing computational complexity and the risk of hallucinating false candidates.
Step 5. NLU-Guided Knowledge Interaction
The framework maintains a domain-specific Knowledge Pack containing structured terminology, entity ontologies, and precomputed embeddings. Functioning as a semantic validator rather than a standard RAG text-retrieval system, the Knowledge Pack is queried based on the NLU predictions. Vector similarity search retrieves the Top-K most semantically compatible entities together with normalized semantic similarity scores, ensuring latency remains extremely low.
Step 6. Adaptive Confusion Index (\text{CI}) Computation
To prevent the universal computation bloat of invoking Audio-LLMs for every utterance, the framework relies on a deterministic gating mechanism. A normalized multi-variate Confusion Index (\text{CI}) is computed by fusing four distinct uncertainty measures:
 * ASR Uncertainty (U_{\text{ASR}}): U_{\text{ASR}} = 1 - \text{Average Token Confidence}.
 * NLU Uncertainty (U_{\text{NLU}}): U_{\text{NLU}} = 1 - \text{Maximum Softmax Probability}.
 * Knowledge Disagreement (U_K): The semantic similarity difference between the highest-ranked and second-highest-ranked retrieved candidates.
 * Context Disagreement (U_C): The cosine similarity divergence between candidate embeddings and recent dialogue history.
These normalized values are fused using a lightweight trainable attention-based module to output a scalar \text{CI} \in [0, 1]. If \text{CI} < \text{Threshold}, the framework proceeds to immediate semantic reconciliation. If \text{CI} \geq \text{Threshold}, the sub-utterance Acoustic Expert is activated.
Step 7. Adaptive Acoustic Expert Invocation (Sub-Utterance Routing)
Rather than routing entire audio sequences to heavy foundation models, this framework isolates localized acoustic failures. Using timestamps from the Whisper decoder, only a short acoustic window (approximately 1–3 seconds surrounding the uncertain token) is extracted. An Audio-LLM (Gemma Audio) is asynchronously invoked solely on this isolated slice to extract structured paralinguistic evidence, including probable technical entities and non-verbal distress cues. This ensures average latency remains close to a cascaded pipeline while surgically applying multimodal reasoning only where acoustic ambiguity demands it.
Step 8. Adaptive Semantic Reconciliation
For every candidate semantic hypothesis, evidence is aggregated into a feature vector consisting of ASR/NLU confidence, knowledge/context similarity scores, and Acoustic Expert evidence (if invoked). Bypassing auto-regressive LLM text generation, these vectors are evaluated via a lightweight Attention-based Evidence Fusion Network. The network architecture (Feature Vector \rightarrow Linear Projection \rightarrow Self-Attention Layer \rightarrow Feed-Forward Layer \rightarrow Sigmoid Output) automatically learns the optimal weight assigned to each evidence source. The candidate achieving the highest final score becomes the corrected semantic representation of the utterance.
Step 9. Final Reasoning and Response Generation
The corrected semantic representation, rather than the raw noisy ASR transcript, is forwarded alongside the conversation history to the downstream LLM. Because the reasoning model operates on this structured, semantically validated input, it avoids Acoustic-to-Semantic Error Cascading while maintaining the high token-generation speed of text-based LLMs. The generated response is synthesized via the selected TTS model, completing an architecture that effectively unites the low latency of cascaded pipelines with the acoustic intelligence of native multimodal models.
